<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aachen Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 796–805</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> Cathedral, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, has as its core the octagonal Palatine Chapel built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> around 796–805, where he was buried in 814. From 936 to 1531 it was the coronation church of German kings, and a Gothic choir was added in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries. It was the first German site on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List, in 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral.jpg" alt="Aachen Cathedral" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aachen_Germany_Imperial-Cathedral-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
